   CA, FEB   25, 1995},
Organization = {Calif Psychol Assoc},
Abstract = {The purpose of this study was ro assess differences in sexually abused
   children's self-concept and experiences of traumatic symptoms and to
   qualitatively examine the manner in which standardized assessment
   instruments could be used by therapists for treatment planning and case
   management. The results of this study suggest that not all sexually
   abused children have the same perceived level of self-concept, social
   support, or traumatic symptoms. Sexually abused children who had high
   self-concept had higher perceived levels of social support. Use of the
   Formal assessments assisted therapists in developing individualized
   treatment plans. Implications of this study are discussed.},
