   Budapest, HUNGARY, AUG, 2013},
Organization = {Fisheries Joint Management Comm; Natl Oceanog \& Atmospher Adm; Natl Sci
   Fdn; NAVFAC; Off Naval Res; Off Naval Res Global; Acoust Soc Amer; Aquat
   Noise Trust; Discovery Sound Sea; Co Biologists; Univ Maryland, Coll
   Chem \& Life Sci; Ctr Comparat \& Evolutionary Biol Hearing},
Abstract = {Marine invertebrates potentially represent a group of species whose
   ecology may be influenced by artificial noise. Exposure to anthropogenic
   sound sources could have a direct consequence on the functionality and
   sensitivity of their sensory organs, the statocysts, which are
   responsible for their equilibrium and movements in the water column. The
   availability of novel laser Doppler vibrometer techniques has recently
   opened the possibility of measuring whole body ( distance, velocity, and
   acceleration) vibration as a direct stimulus eliciting statocyst
   response, offering the scientific community a new level of understanding
   of the marine invertebrate hearing mechanism.},
