Abstract = {In this research, we relate spatial resolution of Digital Elevation
   Model (DEM) and temporal resolution of hyetograph on accuracy of urban
   flood modeling. An approach is devised to determine optimal resolutions
   to calibrate model outputs. Flood models of two urban micro watersheds
   are setup in Geographic Information System (GIS)-framework. Models are
   simulated over selected sites (Blacklot and East Mall) on University of
   Nevada-Las Vegas (UNLV) campus. These sites were flooded from a 25-year
   1-hour rainfall event on September 11, 2012. The model uses DEM from
   Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR) point cloud elevation data and
   rainfall hyetograph at 15-minute sample intervals from a local rainfall
   gage to estimate flooding. Soil Conservation Service (SCS) curve number
   method and head-capacity model of the drain inlets of the study sites
   are coupled in GIS framework to model runoff and flood. Model is first
   simulated for various DEM spatial resolutions (between 1m 10m) to find
   out the optimal DEM, and next, it is simulated for various hyetograph
   temporal resolutions (between 1-min and 15-min) using the optimal DEM.
   The DEM is used to delineate micro watersheds for the drain inlets
   whereas hyetograph is used to estimate runoff and discharge hydrograph.
   The 5-m DEM produces the lowest error (24\%) between the estimated and
   observed peak flood depths in Blacklot while 1m DEM produces the lowest
   error (5\%) in East Mall. Other DEM resolutions underestimate or
   overestimate the flood depth. A rainfall distribution with 15-min
   interval produces the lowest error between the estimated and observed
   peak flood depths. The errors are found greater for other resolution.
   Thus, DEM spatial and hyetograph temporal resolutions have effects on
   flood modeling accuracy. These are important parameters to be considered
   when using in the flood models.},
