Note = {14th International Congress of Legal and Forensic Psychology, Elche,
   SPAIN, MAR 01-02, 2024},
Abstract = {This study aims to describe an educational intervention for juveniles
   serving judicial measures, in which STEM subjects were taught using
   teaching strategies derived from the MUSIC model of academic motivation
   and problem-based learning. This intervention was part of a broader
   research project called STEMPATHY, which aimed to promote interest in
   STEM professions among secondary school students, particularly women.
   The intervention involved designing and building, with the Lego
   Mindstorm kit, a device that would allow people with reduced mobility to
   fully use a tube of toothpaste or to lift and close the toilet lid. The
   effect of the intervention was evaluated with scores obtained before and
   after the intervention, comparing a group of 27 minors serving measures
   of probation and on living in an educational group with a group of 44
   similar-aged minors with no records in the juvenile justice system. The
   measures used included participants' perceptions of people working in
   engineering, and of the facilitators who guided them through the
   educational intervention. Their attitudes toward STEM subjects and their
   teaching, as well as toward science and technology in general, were also
   evaluated. The experience was assessed in relation to the RNR and Good
   Lives models for the offender rehabilitation, the educational nature of
   Organic Law 5/2000 and the socioeconomic context in which it is
   implemented.},
